course: dictionary
lesson: 15574
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Swag is one of them. To find the right meaning, read the words around it. «She walked into the party with loads of swag» is about style and confidence, but «The burglar ran off with a bag full of swag» is about stolen goods.
explain: «The conference gave every visitor a bag of swag: pens, stickers and a badge.» Here swag means free things given away at an event. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, then put your meaning in place of the word to check it still makes sense.
explain: Some meanings belong to certain places. In Australia, «He unrolled his swag under the gum tree» means a bedroll, a canvas bag with a mattress for sleeping outdoors. When one word has several meanings, the context is the only clue that tells you which one you need.
fact: Swag can mean cool style and confidence, as in «He walked in with loads of swag.» (slang)
fact: Swag can mean free things given away at events, such as badges, pens, stickers and T-shirts. (informal)
fact: Swag can mean stolen goods, especially what a burglar carries away in a bag. (old slang)
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, a swag is a bedroll, and a swagman was a traveller who carried one.
fact: Swag can also mean a piece of cloth or a decoration hanging in a curve, as above a window.
exercise: «He walked into the party with loads of swag.» What does swag mean here? | Stolen goods | A bedroll for sleeping outdoors | *Cool style and confidence
exercise: «The conference gave every visitor a bag of swag: pens, stickers and a badge.» What is swag here? | A bedroll | A curtain | *Free things given away at an event | Stolen goods
exercise: «The burglar ran off with a bag full of swag.» What does swag mean? | Free badges | *Stolen goods | Cool style | A piece of cloth hanging in a curve
test: «Under the gum tree, the swagman unrolled his swag and slept outdoors.» What is a swag in this sentence? | Free stickers | Cool style and confidence | Stolen goods | *A bedroll, a canvas bag with a mattress
test: «The curtains were tied back with a swag of red cloth.» What does swag mean here? | *A piece of cloth hanging in a curve | Free T-shirts | A bedroll | Stolen goods
exam: Mia says swag only means stolen goods. Is she right? | Yes, because burglars always use the word | *No, swag has several meanings, and the context shows which one | Yes, swag always means stolen goods | No, swag only means cool style
